<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankopan family</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Frankopans were a Croatian noble family, originally counts of the island of Krk, who from the 12th century held large estates in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and along the coast. Among the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> of medieval Croatia, they produced bans, built castles and churches and supported Glagolitic literacy. The family ended in 1671 when Fran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krsto-frankopan">Krsto Frankopan</a> was executed after the Magnate conspiracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankopan-family.jpg" alt="Frankopan family" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Siebmacher - vereinzelt und bearbeitet von Dorado · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frangepan-2Wappen_Sm.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first known member of the line was Dujam I Krčki, lord of Krk, whom Domenico Michieli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a> from 1118 to 1130, allowed to rule the island as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. His origin is unknown, and his descendants appear in the documents as the Counts of Krk. In 1428 Nikola IV Krčki, Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, was the first to call himself Frankapan, and in 1430 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> recognised the family as descendants of the Roman patrician Frangipani, a link that modern reference works such as the Croatian Encyclopedia strongly question.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1221 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a> gave the brothers Henry and Servidon the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brac">Brač</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hvar">Hvar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lastovo">Lastovo</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1240–1241, the family sheltered King Béla IV and supplied him with arms and money, and he rewarded them with the county of Senj and the castle of Modruš. In 1294 Nikola Frankopan rebuilt the Holy House of Our Lady at Trsat. In 1425 Emperor Sigismund confirmed the noble status of Nikola Frankopan as Count of Krk, Senj and Modruš and granted the family the right to seal in red wax.</p><p>Over the following centuries the Frankopans and the Zrinski family, joined by many marriages, led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> absolutism. Several Frankopans served as bans of Croatia, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krsto-frankopan">Krsto Frankopan</a> and Franjo Frankopan. Fran Krsto Frankopan took part in the conspiracy against King Leopold I and was executed with his brother-in-law Petar Zrinski at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiener-neustadt">Wiener Neustadt</a>. With him the Trsat branch died out in 1671, the other branches having ended in the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many Frankopan castles survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, mostly in the Gorski kotar region and on Krk, at places such as Bosiljevo, Severin na Kupi, Novi Vinodolski, Ogulin, Slunj, Ozalj and Trsat; the castle in the town of Krk serves for open-air performances in summer. In 1991 Louis Doimi de Lupis, a British citizen, adopted the names Frankopan, Šubić and Zrinski, which a cousin publicly disputed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frankopan family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great families such as the Šubići of Bribir and the <b>Frankopans</b> held vast lands; Ban Paul I Šubić was almost independent around 1300.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526)</a></li></ul></section>
